    q4: "‘In the medieval period (c1250–c1500), ideas about the cause of illness were mainly based on religion.’ How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks + 4 marks SPaG]<br><br>Ideas about the cause of illness in the medieval period were heavily dominated by religious beliefs, reflecting a deeply pious society where the Catholic Church held an ideological monopoly over education, healthcare, and everyday life. However, I agree with the statement only to a partial extent; alongside divine explanations, medieval society possessed an equally influential, rational framework based on the Greco-Roman Theory of the Four Humours and the environmental doctrine of miasma.<br><br>On the one hand, religious explanations were indeed the primary framework through which ordinary people understood illness. The medieval Catholic Church taught that God was omnipotent and that illness was sent directly by God as divine retribution for sin, a moral test of faith, or collective chastisement. During the 1348 Black Death, which killed up to half the population, priests preached that the epidemic was God's wrath. This supernatural diagnosis shaped everyday responses: thousands joined flagellant brotherhoods, communities held religious processions, and sick individuals confessed sins, bought indulgences, and made arduous pilgrimages to Canterbury. Furthermore, the Church strictly policed intellectual enquiry: monks in scriptoria copied only texts that conformed to scripture, and scholars who advocated empirical observation—such as Roger Bacon in 1277—were imprisoned for heresy.<br><br>On the other hand, university-trained physicians and educated elites relied extensively on secular, naturalistic explanations inherited from classical antiquity. Hippocrates and Galen’s **Theory of the Four Humours** (blood, phlegm, black bile, yellow bile) asserted that disease was caused by an internal biological imbalance resulting from diet, weather, or lifestyle rather than supernatural anger. Physicians diagnosed humoural disharmony using urine flasks (matula) and restored balance through physical interventions like bloodletting (phlebotomy) and purging. Crucially, the Church actively promoted Galen because he argued the body was designed by a purposeful Creator; thus, rational humoural theory was officially integrated into university training alongside theology.<br><br>Furthermore, medieval people possessed a widespread environmental theory of disease based on **miasma (corrupt air)**. Physicians believed decaying filth, cesspits, unburied corpses, and volcanic fissures generated poisonous vapours that corrupted the atmosphere and disrupted the humours when inhaled. During epidemics, town authorities cleaned open dung heaps, regulated butcher waste, and citizens burned sweet-smelling herbs to purify the air. Additionally, medieval astrology (the alignment of planets and the Zodiac Man) was considered an empirical science; the 1345 conjunction of Saturn, Jupiter, and Mars was widely cited as a natural catalyst that corrupted the air.<br><br>In conclusion, while religion provided the ultimate spiritual explanation for why human suffering existed, ideas about illness were not based solely on religion. Rather, medieval medical thinking was a synthesis: people believed God sent disease as a spiritual catalyst, but understood that disease physically operated through natural mechanisms—specifically humoural imbalance and miasma."
