    q4: "‘Approaches to dealing with the Great Plague in 1665 were completely different from approaches to the Black Death in 1348.’ How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks + 4 marks SPaG]<br><br>While there were notable differences in the organisation and legal enforcement of government measures in 1665, I agree only to a partial extent that approaches were completely different; fundamentally, both societies relied on the same underlying theories of disease causation—miasma and divine punishment—which meant their therapeutic treatments and daily protective responses remained remarkably similar.<br><br>On the one hand, approaches in 1665 demonstrated significant administrative advancement and systematic government organization compared to 1348. During the Great Plague, the Mayor and Aldermen of London enforced structured municipal 'Plague Orders'. For the first time, infected households were quarantined for 28 days behind locked doors with a red cross painted on the entrance and the words 'Lord have mercy upon us', guarded day and night by armed watchmen. Parishes appointed dedicated 'searchers of the dead' to inspect corpses and record plague fatalities in official weekly **Bills of Mortality**. Furthermore, public gatherings, taverns, and theatres were shut down, and authorities ordered the mass culling of over 40,000 stray dogs and 200,000 cats. In contrast, in 1348, medieval government was fragmented; King Edward III did little beyond ordering the Mayor to clean streets of animal offal, and local councils lacked the legal machinery to impose comprehensive household quarantines.<br><br>However, despite these administrative differences, the fundamental understanding of what caused the plague had not changed at all in 317 years, leading to almost identical preventive practices. In both 1348 and 1665, people believed the plague was caused by **corrupted miasma (poisonous air)** or divine wrath sent by God to punish sinners. Consequently, the methods used by ordinary people were virtually unchanged: in 1665, citizens carried sweet-smelling pomanders, held posies of herbs to their noses, and burned aromatic sulfur and pitch barrels in the streets, just as medieval citizens had carried lavender and burned tar in 1348. The King ordered nationwide days of fasting and prayer to appease God's anger, echoing fourteenth-century religious processions.<br><br>Furthermore, medical treatments in 1665 remained almost identical to medieval medicine because physicians still had no concept of bacteria or flea-and-rat vectors. Plague doctors continued to lance agonizing buboes to release corrupted humours, applied live plucked pigeons to swellings to 'draw out poison', and bled patients using leeches. Wealthy citizens—including King Charles II, Parliament, and university-trained physicians—responded to the outbreak by fleeing London for the countryside, leaving poor citizens trapped in slums exactly as medieval lords had abandoned infected towns in 1348.<br><br>In conclusion, although the 1665 response was far more bureaucratic and legally coordinated through official quarantine orders and Bills of Mortality, approaches were not completely different. Because neither society understood that *Yersinia pestis* was transmitted by rodent fleas, the core medical treatments, preventive habits, and reliance on prayer and flight remained fundamentally identical to 1348."
